```

Run `selftest` right after installing: it builds a throwaway folder, runs 13 checks (new-item detection, one wake per threshold crossing, bad numbers, damaged or cut-off or slow responses, scripts that hang or fail, a secret in script output, three failures in a row, recovery) and must end with `selftest: 13 of 13 passed`. If it does not, tell the user and stop; do not convert anything.

Use it as `python3 /workspace/script-it/sit.py <command>`. Data lives in `~/.script-it` (or `SCRIPT_IT_HOME`): `watches.json` (+ `.bak`), `state/`, `changes.jsonl`, `run.log`, `scripts/` (scripts the user approved), optional `secrets.env`. Any line starting `ERROR:` ends with `Fix:`; pass it on in plain words and don't repeat the same failing call more than once.

What it does and does not do: sources are an https address (or localhost), ONE absolute file path, or a script the user approved that sits in `scripts/`. Scripts run with python3, no shell, a timeout, and only the secrets named in `env_names`. Redirects are not followed. Responses and files over 2 MB, cut-off responses and invalid JSON count as a failed check, not a crash. Changes keep a 200-character summary; webhook posts are under 1 KB. Tested only on Linux with a local sample server: do not claim it works on Windows or macOS, with long-term cron, or with any live bot platform's webhooks until it has been tried there. Measured on one Linux machine (median of 7, Python startup included): about 0.04 s of CPU for one pass over 5 file watches and 0.04 s for 50. Web addresses and scripts cost more and were not measured.
